WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.090
Fábio runs the back office assistant at a distributor.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.935
The agent reconciles payments, issues invoices, checks stock, opens tickets with

00:00:10.935 --> 00:00:14.401
carriers, and answers questions about commissions.

00:00:15.051 --> 00:00:19.495
After merging the duplicate tools last quarter, thirty-four of them remained.

00:00:19.795 --> 00:00:21.983
All legitimate, all different.

00:00:22.283 --> 00:00:24.729
And someone uses every one of them.

00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:27.592
But the agent still makes mistakes.

00:00:27.892 --> 00:00:33.416
On a reconciliation request, it calls check stock, instead of find payment.

00:00:33.716 --> 00:00:37.755
Both take an order code, and their descriptions start the same way.

00:00:38.055 --> 00:00:42.824
The trace shows the model hesitating between tools that shouldn't even be on the

00:00:42.824 --> 00:00:43.182
table.

00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:44.936
And there's the bill.

00:00:45.236 --> 00:00:48.831
Every call carries all thirty-four definitions.

00:00:49.131 --> 00:00:52.712
In a six turn loop, they go to the model six times.

00:00:53.362 --> 00:00:55.074
Fábio can't remove tools.

00:00:55.374 --> 00:00:59.309
What he can change is how many the model sees at a time.

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:00.687
That's this lesson.

00:01:01.887 --> 00:01:04.589
Organize the tools into groups by topic.

00:01:04.889 --> 00:01:11.340
Reconciliation, invoices, stock, and logistics, each in its own group.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:17.234
Before the agent loop starts, a short step decides which groups this task needs.

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:20.453
And the main agent runs with only those groups.

00:01:21.103 --> 00:01:25.917
The key move is taking one choice away from the big model, and putting it in a

00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:27.645
small, cheap, testable step.

00:01:27.945 --> 00:01:32.656
The main agent stops answering which of these thirty-four, and starts answering

00:01:32.656 --> 00:01:33.908
which of these eight.

00:01:34.208 --> 00:01:35.979
And there's always a way back.

00:01:36.279 --> 00:01:40.770
If the agent discovers it needs another group, it can ask for it.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.589
The task arrives, as a person's text or a system event.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:51.334
A small model reads the task, and the list of groups, one description line each.

00:01:51.984 --> 00:01:55.852
The catalog stores the groups and each group's tools, versioned.

00:01:56.502 --> 00:02:00.459
The router returns the chosen groups, and its confidence.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:05.761
The main agent runs with those groups' tools, plus one extra tool, to ask for

00:02:05.761 --> 00:02:06.606
another group.

00:02:07.256 --> 00:02:09.271
Tools execute as usual.

00:02:09.921 --> 00:02:13.700
And the answer goes out, with the trace recording which groups were used.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.229
A task comes in.

00:02:16.529 --> 00:02:20.875
Reconcile the payment for order eighty-eight two thirty-one with yesterday's

00:02:20.875 --> 00:02:23.506
statement, and if it matches, settle the bill.

00:02:23.806 --> 00:02:27.498
The router gets the task, and four lines, one per group.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:32.847
Billing: use to reconcile payments against statements, and settle bills.

00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:37.878
Invoices, stock and logistics, each with its own use.

00:02:38.528 --> 00:02:42.742
It returns: billing, with ninety-two percent confidence.

00:02:43.042 --> 00:02:47.797
The main agent runs with the seven billing tools, plus the one to request a group.

00:02:48.097 --> 00:02:51.470
The stock tool that caused the confusion never shows up.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:55.477
And a tool that isn't on the list can't be picked by mistake.

00:02:56.127 --> 00:02:58.974
Now the math, with illustrative numbers.

00:02:59.274 --> 00:03:03.590
Say each tool definition takes about two hundred fifty tokens.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.147
The thirty-four add up to roughly eight thousand five hundred tokens per call.

00:03:08.797 --> 00:03:13.905
Over a six turn loop, that's about fifty-one thousand tokens of definitions alone.

00:03:14.555 --> 00:03:18.070
With eight tools, the same task carries about twelve thousand.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.288
The router costs one short call on a small model.

00:03:21.588 --> 00:03:22.786
It pays once.

00:03:23.086 --> 00:03:25.492
The savings happen on every turn.

00:03:26.692 --> 00:03:27.856
Here's one for you.

00:03:28.156 --> 00:03:33.025
A week after launching the router, the dashboard shows that in forty percent of

00:03:33.025 --> 00:03:37.032
collections tasks, the agent asks for the invoices group mid-run.

00:03:37.332 --> 00:03:39.002
What's the most direct fix?

00:03:42.052 --> 00:03:47.578
Include invoices in the initial selection for collections tasks, by adjusting the

00:03:47.578 --> 00:03:50.102
router's description or its examples.

00:03:50.402 --> 00:03:56.040
A group requested often, for the same task type, means the initial selection is too

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:56.516
narrow.

00:03:56.816 --> 00:04:00.164
Expansion is an escape hatch, not the normal route.

00:04:00.814 --> 00:04:05.665
Removing the request tool, because it makes the agent slow, would leave those tasks

00:04:05.665 --> 00:04:07.010
without the right tool.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.999
And merging all groups back into one undoes the pattern.

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:15.781
Collections and invoices show up together, so they go in together.

00:04:16.981 --> 00:04:21.136
If the agent has few tools, consolidate first.

00:04:21.436 --> 00:04:23.141
That was the last lesson.

00:04:23.791 --> 00:04:28.943
If tasks cross every topic all the time, the router would always pick everything.

00:04:29.593 --> 00:04:34.543
If your provider offers tool search on demand, and your only problem is token cost,

00:04:34.543 --> 00:04:37.943
that mechanism solves it, without an extra step of yours.

00:04:38.593 --> 00:04:44.040
And if the input channel already tells you the topic, like a queue or an event type,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.661
use a fixed rule in code.

00:04:45.961 --> 00:04:50.084
No model, zero cost, and zero classification errors.

00:04:50.734 --> 00:04:52.324
Now the common traps.

00:04:52.624 --> 00:04:55.191
Grouping by system, not by task.

00:04:55.491 --> 00:05:00.018
Everything that talks to the management system becomes a twenty-five tool group.

00:05:00.318 --> 00:05:02.314
Group by what the person wants to do.

00:05:02.614 --> 00:05:05.737
Like reconcile, issue, or ship an order.

00:05:06.387 --> 00:05:08.541
Describing contents, not use.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.716
Billing tools doesn't help the router.

00:05:11.016 --> 00:05:16.158
Use to reconcile payments against statements does, because it matches how tasks

00:05:16.158 --> 00:05:16.614
arrive.

00:05:17.264 --> 00:05:19.545
A default set with side effects.

00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:22.530
Low confidence means the router is unsure.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.111
The safe default is read only.

00:05:25.411 --> 00:05:27.427
Not everything, just in case.

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:29.807
And forgetting the escape hatch.

00:05:30.107 --> 00:05:35.038
Without a way to request a group, a router mistake becomes an agent that answers

00:05:35.038 --> 00:05:36.456
without the right tool.

00:05:36.756 --> 00:05:38.426
Or makes up the data.

00:05:39.626 --> 00:05:43.320
The most expensive failure here is the silent one.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.708
The right group was left out, and the agent didn't ask.

00:05:47.008 --> 00:05:50.687
It answers I couldn't find the payment, with total calm.

00:05:50.987 --> 00:05:53.261
That's why coverage matters most.

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:59.342
In how many tasks do the chosen groups include every tool the correct solution uses?

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:03.683
But coverage and size pull in opposite directions.

00:06:03.983 --> 00:06:08.868
A router that always picks everything has perfect coverage, and is useless.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.283
Track both together.

00:06:10.933 --> 00:06:13.316
And watch the expansion rate per group.

00:06:13.616 --> 00:06:18.315
A group requested often should join the initial selection for that task type.

00:06:18.965 --> 00:06:23.552
You can build the router's dataset from traces you already have.

00:06:23.852 --> 00:06:28.932
For every task solved successfully, the expected groups are the groups of the tools

00:06:28.932 --> 00:06:30.523
the agent actually called.

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:35.863
Include tasks that cross topics on purpose, like issue the invoice and book the

00:06:35.863 --> 00:06:36.309
pickup.

00:06:36.609 --> 00:06:39.985
And vague ones, like see what happened with this order.

00:06:40.285 --> 00:06:43.565
And when you add a new tool, rerun the router's eval.

00:06:43.865 --> 00:06:48.844
A changed group description can divert tasks that used to go to the right place.

00:06:49.494 --> 00:06:51.066
And one security point.

00:06:51.366 --> 00:06:53.211
The router shrinks the surface.

00:06:53.511 --> 00:06:56.735
Side effect tools only appear on routes that need them.

00:06:57.035 --> 00:07:01.169
A question about delivery dates has no way to trigger a refund.

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:04.023
But don't treat it as access control.

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:09.794
Malicious text inside the task can talk the router into a different group, and the

00:07:09.794 --> 00:07:12.130
agent can ask for groups by itself.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:15.540
Real permission lives in the tool and in the backend.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.354
Who the user is, what they can do, and the amount limit.

00:07:20.554 --> 00:07:23.024
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:23.324 --> 00:07:28.534
When every tool is legitimate but no task needs them all, choose the toolbox before

00:07:28.534 --> 00:07:29.538
the work starts.

00:07:29.838 --> 00:07:34.699
A small step picks the groups, the agent works with a short list and a way to ask

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:37.879
for more, and you measure coverage and size together.

00:07:38.529 --> 00:07:43.773
In the next lesson, we move from the tools to the conversation itself, and what

00:07:43.773 --> 00:07:46.229
happens as it gets long: context rot.

00:07:46.529 --> 00:07:47.319
See you there.
